.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
.TH OCTA-AGGREGATE 1 "" "OCTA-CAPTURE @PROJECT_VERSION@" "OCTA-CAPTURE Manual"
.SH NAME
octa\-aggregate \- prepare an experimental base/EXP ALSA configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBocta-aggregate \-\-base\fR ID \fB\-\-expansion\fR ID [\fB\-\-write-config\fR FILE]
               [\fB\-\-bin-dir\fR DIR] [\fB\-\-help\fR]
               [\fB\-\-rate\fR HZ \fB\-\-experimental\fR]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Inspect one base device (0582:0120) and one EXP device (0582:0121) by discovered identity,
for example serial:SERIAL. Emit a JSON plan after two matching clock snapshots
and repeated identity checks. Run as the regular user.
.PP
Both devices must already use the same 44100, 48000 or 96000 Hz rate.
Connect EXP COAXIAL OUT to base COAXIAL IN and enable DIGITAL AUTO on the base.
The base must report an active external-clock display. These flags do not prove clock quality.
.PP
With \fB\-\-write-config\fR, create a new mode-0600 ALSA configuration file.
Existing files and symlinks are refused. The PCM name is octa_expand:
24 capture channels, 20 playback channels, base channels first, EXP poll master,
S24_3LE with no resampling. Set ALSA_CONFIG_PATH to this file when opening the PCM.
.PP
Without \fB\-\-rate\fR, inspection/configuration does not change the hardware rate,
desktop profiles or ~/.asoundrc. Other clients must release both devices before
using the aggregate PCM. Regenerate after clock changes, unplug or card renumbering.
.SH JOINT RATE CHANGE
With \fB\-\-rate\fR and explicit \fB\-\-experimental\fR, require an internally
clocked EXP master and jointly change both units. Select both by distinct serials.
Native PulseAudio clients on both cards move to temporary endpoints before either
card is released. Device serials must agree with PulseAudio/udev identities.
The native octa-pair-clock helper changes only EXP; both clocks and both restored
stereo profiles are verified before client routes, levels and defaults resume.
Headless use performs the same native clock operation without creating audio-server endpoints.
.PP
This mode installs device-specific stereo profiles and updates their private
ALSA definitions and ~/.asoundrc under a shared configuration lock. Remap chains,
their independent gains and playback-monitor recordings are preserved. Remaps
spanning different stereo pairs must be unloaded or reconfigured; multichannel
clients and module-owned audio streams must be stopped first. No channel mapping
is guessed. Direct ALSA clients must
release their streams themselves. PipeWire coordination is not supported yet.
.PP
On failure, restore both saved configurations in reverse order and verify the old
paired clock before restoring either card's clients. Failed recovery retains
temporary endpoints; it does not deliberately rescue clients to unrelated outputs.
SIGINT/SIGTERM request recovery. The helper gets 75 seconds, then SIGTERM and up
to 45 seconds for recovery before forced termination. Forced termination does
not prove restoration. A new --write-config file is prepared before audio changes
and removed on failure if its inode still belongs to this command; existing files
are never overwritten. Successful commands emit the final plan.
.PP
Joint changes have fixed process/filesystem/audio-graph tests, but real two-unit
PulseAudio operation, physical synchronization, streaming and recovery remain
unverified. Explicit paired GUI launches use the same joint clock coordinator
while controlling each unit individually; they do not create the aggregate PCM.

.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success, 130 an interrupted transaction, and other nonzero values
a usage, device or operation error.
.SH SEE ALSO
octa-capture(1), octa-pair-clock(1), octa-status(1)
